lessons.learnMarkdown:
## Components with inputs

A component is a function, and **props** are its arguments. Whatever attributes you write on the tag arrive as one object:

```jsx
<Avatar user="ByteRunner" size={64} online={true} />

function Avatar(props) {
  // props = { user: "ByteRunner", size: 64, online: true }
}
```

Strings can use quotes. Anything else (numbers, booleans, arrays, objects, functions) goes in braces. `size="64"` would pass the *string* `"64"`, which is a classic bug.

### Destructuring props

Most React code unpacks props right in the parameter list, using **destructuring**:

```jsx
function Avatar({ user, size, online = false }) {
  return <img src={`/avatars/${user}.png`} width={size} alt={user} />;
}
```

`online = false` is a **default value**: it's used when the prop isn't passed at all.

### The children prop

Whatever you put *between* a component's opening and closing tags arrives as `children`:

```jsx
<Panel title="Tips">Drink water. Take breaks.</Panel>

function Panel({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <section>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      {children}
    </section>
  );
}
```

That makes wrapper components (cards, panels, layouts) easy to reuse.

### One-way data flow

Props flow **down** from parent to child. A component must never change its props: treat them as read-only. (When data needs to change, that's **state**, coming up soon.) Because data only flows one way, you can always find where a value came from by looking up the tree.

### New words

- **Props** (*properties*): the input object a component receives from its parent.
- **Destructuring**: unpacking object properties into variables: `const { a, b } = obj`.
- **Default value**: a fallback used when a prop isn't passed: `{ badge = "New" }`.
- **children**: the special prop holding whatever is between a component's tags.
- **One-way data flow**: data passes from parent to child through props, never back up.

